feat(hooks): drive the three ingestion tools from a second harness, then pin their names (#209)

E11 added describe_workspace, observe_tool_call and observe_session and rewrote
the reference shim onto them. None of that demonstrated anything on its own:
the tools were extracted from that harness, so that harness was always going to
fit them. This is the measurement that turns "harness-neutral" into a fact.

The second harness is Codex CLI, which has no hook system this shim can use. It
writes one JSONL rollout per session, so the route is post-hoc transcript
ingestion -- session_meta opens the run, each custom_tool_call and function_call
is forwarded, the end of the file retires it. That is the weakest assumption
available and therefore the strongest proof: it is what a harness that
cooperates in no way must do, and anything the MCP still left a caller to work
out for itself would have surfaced at once.

Driven by OPS-022 and OPS-023, both written and watched failing first. OPS-022
started at six failing assertions with nothing on the wire; OPS-023 was red
because renaming observe_tool_call passed in silence -- the gate knew five
names, reported "mcp-tool 5 5 OK", and the pin was not live. After the pin the
same rename is caught twice, as a partial set and as drift.

Measured end to end against a live deployment, on a throwaway repository: a run
with identity, three tool calls, a credential, an intent, a signed commit at
Rekor index 532 and a retirement, in that order -- the same event sequence a
reference-harness run produces, with the same source and schema_version, and
bodies on the operator's volume digest-named and 0600. The commit verifies from
a container with no route to the ledger: Fulcio chain, Rekor inclusion proof,
trailer matching the certificate identity.

There is no commit refusal on this route and none was invented. A transcript is
read after the session, so by the time the shim sees a commit it exists. What is
lost is exact: an unattributed commit becomes possible, attribution survives
because the call is in the transcript, and enforcement moves from commit time to
merge time where scripts/verify-branch.sh still refuses it.

The shim is 15 executable lines of read-and-forward against a 30-line target,
plus 26 of MCP transport that no tool can remove and 11 of git plumbing for the
stop-time capture. Those 11 lines are reported rather than hidden: the reference
shim has the same three commands, and a second shim needing them independently
is what says the capture belongs in the MCP next.

The stub MCP moved out of shim-selftest.sh into scripts/hooks/stub-mcp.py so
both self-tests drive one copy. Copying seventy lines into a second test would
have been, in the suite that proves E11, the exact duplication E11 answers.

ADR-0048 records the harness, the route, the limitation and the line count.

